# Ignore rules scoped to the slatwall-ts/ subtree. Git applies every pattern below relative
# to THIS directory, so nothing here reaches the legacy CFML tree.
#
# That scoping is the reason the file exists. The repository-root .gitignore covers the legacy
# application and not one of its fifteen entries covers node_modules, dist, build-meta or
# coverage; closing that gap here rather than there keeps the root file, and the whole CFML tree,
# byte-for-byte unchanged, so this port stays a single additive diff.
#
# The list is deliberately short — FIVE patterns and nothing else, and the count has now been
# wrong twice, so it is stated against the file rather than from memory: the FOUR generated
# directories node_modules/, dist/, build-meta/ and coverage/, plus the single file pattern
# .env at the end. An earlier version of this note said three and listed four; the version
# after it said four while build-meta/ was already present, making five. Count the patterns
# below before editing this sentence. Speculative boilerplate is omitted rather than copied
# in (AAP 0.7.3 standard 9, guideline 0.8.2/1). The four DIRECTORY names are shared with
# tsconfig.json "exclude", tsconfig.build.json "outDir" and "exclude", eslint.config.mjs
# "ignores", jest.config.ts "coverageDirectory" and "testPathIgnorePatterns", and the output,
# staging and sourcemap directories in build/esbuild.mjs. Every one of those must keep saying
# the same words, or a build product ends up tracked — or, as a QA pass found for build-meta/
# in eslint.config.mjs, ends up LINTED.
#
# Nothing else belongs here, and the omissions are decisions rather than oversights. The
# fifth pattern is the only credential-bearing entry, and it is deliberate: a real .env
# may hold live database credentials, whereas configuration otherwise reaches the service
# only through the process environment (AAP 0.4.3.5, 0.8.3.9) and no file in this subtree
# holds a credential. The committed .env.example carries variable NAMES only — the required ones
# blank, the optional ones commented out, and no value of any kind — and is therefore tracked on
# purpose; see the note on the .env entry below. No editor, OS or tool-cache pattern is listed:
# each would be boilerplate for an artifact this subtree never emits.

# Installed dependency tree, materialised by `npm ci` from package-lock.json. The
# lockfile itself is deliberately NOT ignored: a committed lockfile is what makes the
# exact-version resolution reproducible (AAP 0.7.3 standard 5).
node_modules/

# Compiled and bundled output. Both routes to an artifact emit here — `tsc -p
# tsconfig.build.json` (outDir "dist") and `node build/esbuild.mjs` (the bundles) — and
# build products are never committed. `dist/` is the PACKAGED tree: after a build it holds
# exactly one CommonJS artifact per declared Lambda entry point and nothing else.
dist/

# Source maps for those bundles, emitted beside `dist/` rather than into it so the packaged
# tree carries only the artifacts the runtime loads. `build/esbuild.mjs` writes them here
# with `sourcemap: 'external'` — no `sourceMappingURL` comment is written into the
# JavaScript, so the maps are relocatable without leaving a dangling reference. Generated
# output like the directory above, and never committed.
#
# IT ALSO HOLDS THE PACKAGE WHILE IT IS BEING ASSEMBLED. `build/esbuild.mjs` writes every
# artifact — the six bundles, the production manifest and the staged runtime dependency
# closure — into `build-meta/package-staging/` and then creates `dist/` with a single
# `rename` of that directory, so a build that fails at any step leaves `dist/` ABSENT rather
# than stale or partial. The staging directory is a sibling of `dist/` for a concrete reason:
# `rename` is atomic only within one filesystem, so a system temporary directory would not do.
# It normally exists only for the duration of a build; the failure handler removes it, and this
# pattern is what guarantees a hard crash cannot leave it tracked.
#
# NOT to be confused with `build/`, which holds the build script itself and IS tracked. The
# names are deliberately distinct for that reason.
build-meta/

# Jest coverage reports (jest.config.ts, coverageDirectory: 'coverage').
coverage/

# THIS FILE IS ALSO PRETTIER'S IGNORE LIST, AND IT IS NOW THE ONLY ONE: no .prettierignore
# exists in this subtree. Prettier's CLI defaults `--ignore-path` to
# `[.gitignore, .prettierignore]` — its own `prettier --help` on 3.9.6 says so verbatim —
# and it skips whichever of the two is absent, so the directories above are already
# excluded from `npx prettier --check .` without a second list to keep in sync.
#
# HISTORY, READ OUT OF `git log --follow --name-status` RATHER THAN RECALLED, because every
# earlier version of this note got some of it wrong. The file's whole life is three commits:
#
#   c747b7455  A  added by the setup commit, alongside eleven other toolchain files
#   ef7f10c79  D  deleted — the commit that also wrote the "no .prettierignore exists" notes
#                 here, in eslint.config.mjs, in jest.config.ts and in build/esbuild.mjs
#   1c93bd295  A  RE-ADDED, carrying a header arguing it was "kept rather than deleted"
#
# So those four notes were TRUE when they were written and were falsified afterwards by a
# commit that restored the file without revisiting them. That is the failure mode worth
# remembering: the notes were not careless, they were duplicated — four copies of one claim,
# and re-adding one file made all four wrong at once. The review recorded the resulting
# disagreement between configuration and repository state as a scope finding. The file has
# now been removed again, and this file — the owning declaration for what is ignored — is the
# single place that records it, so the three sibling notes point here rather than restating
# anything that a future commit could falsify a second time.
#
# WHY REMOVAL IS SAFE, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. With the file absent, deliberately
# mis-formatted probe files were written to dist/, to coverage/ and to src/, and then
# `npx prettier --check .` was run once: it reported ONLY the src/ probe. That single run
# proves both halves of the claim — that the two generated directories really are excluded,
# and that the scan really was happening rather than silently matching nothing. The removed
# list named one further path, package-lock.json, and `npx prettier package-lock.json`
# output is byte-identical to the file on disk, so checking the lockfile is a no-op rather
# than a churn risk. Do not re-add the file to silence a hypothetical: measure first,
# exactly as this note did.

# A real .env is a local developer convenience that may hold live database credentials,
# so it never enters git; configuration reaches the service through the environment and
# no credential is hardcoded (AAP 0.8.3.9). The pattern is the exact filename and not a
# wildcard, so the required, committed .env.example — variable names only, no values —
# is never swallowed.
.env
